<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The chiefdom of Hawaiʻi Island, largest of the Hawaiian islands, from which Kamehameha I launched the conquests that created the Kingdom of Hawaii.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</a>, son of the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a>, overthrew his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakau">Hākau</a> and united the island's six districts under one aliʻi nui. Tradition credits him with reorganising land into ahupuaʻa, wedge-shaped units running from mountain to sea, and building stone temples. His successors ruled from royal centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waipio-valley">Waipiʻo Valley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailua-kona">Kailua-Kona</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The aliʻi nui stood at the head of a hierarchy of chiefs, priests and land managers enforced by the kapu system, and the island's dryland field systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kona-hawaii">Kona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kohala-hawaii">Kohala</a> fed a large population. Rival lines fought for the title. The ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a> in 1779, and Cook was killed there in a clash that February.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> died in 1782, his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kiwalaʻō</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Mokuʻōhai</a> and, after years of war, won the whole island by 1791. Conquering the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-maui">Chiefdom of Maui</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-oahu">Chiefdom of Oahu</a>, he founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii</a> in 1795.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:5.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>ʻUmi-a-Līloa, Kalaniʻōpuʻu, Kamehameha I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hawaiian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Hawaiʻi Island, Hawaiʻi Island chiefdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1648&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-hawaii-island" aria-label="Chiefdom of Hawaii Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2274 754 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chiefdom of Hawaii Island lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2594 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2594 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1648&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
